WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.650
Hello, everyone.

00:00:01.650 --> 00:00:04.950
I'm Daniela Rus,
and I make robots.

00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:08.460
I'm a robotics researcher
at MIT's Computer Science

00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:12.090
and Artificial Intelligence
Laboratory, or CSAIL, and also

00:00:12.090 --> 00:00:13.650
the director of CSAIL.

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:17.460
And today I will be
moderating this panel

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:20.200
on the future of computing.

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:23.190
So in this panel, we
hope to explore with you

00:00:23.190 --> 00:00:27.480
some of our latest
insights and the challenges

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:29.790
that our researchers
are tackling

00:00:29.790 --> 00:00:31.740
in the field of computing.

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:36.780
We also will discuss what the
future of the digital landscape

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:40.830
might look like and how
computer science research will

00:00:40.830 --> 00:00:43.500
make a difference in
how we live, we work,

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:46.620
and we play in the future.

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:49.870
I will tell you more about
CSAIL in just a minute.

00:00:49.870 --> 00:00:53.670
First, I would like to
introduce our panelists.

00:00:53.670 --> 00:00:56.600
Tim Berners-Lee probably
needs no introduction,

00:00:56.600 --> 00:01:02.130
but he invented the world
wide web 25 years ago in 1989

00:01:02.130 --> 00:01:06.330
when he was a researcher at
CERN, which is the European

00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:08.940
Particle Physics Laboratory.

00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:13.320
And today Tim is a crusader
for a world wide web that

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:16.630
is of even greater
benefit to humanity

00:01:16.630 --> 00:01:21.150
to easy access to information
and through providing

00:01:21.150 --> 00:01:24.350
more transparency on
how governments operate.

00:01:39.350 --> 00:01:42.440
Andrew Lo was named
by Time Magazine

00:01:42.440 --> 00:01:44.540
one of the most
influential people

00:01:44.540 --> 00:01:50.960
of the year for his innovative
approach to financial markets.

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:53.810
And Lo is an
economist by training.

00:01:53.810 --> 00:01:57.650
He is a faculty member at MIT
Sloan School of Management.

00:01:57.650 --> 00:02:01.900
He directs the laboratory
for financial engineering,

00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:04.405
and he's also principle
investigator at CSAIL.

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:15.380
And Russ Tedrake
can make robots that

00:02:15.380 --> 00:02:19.460
fly like birds,
run like ostriches,

00:02:19.460 --> 00:02:23.090
and these days, he's directing
his innovative approaches

00:02:23.090 --> 00:02:27.650
to creating robots that can
assist in search and rescue.

00:02:27.650 --> 00:02:30.740
Russ finds his
inspiration in nature,

00:02:30.740 --> 00:02:32.930
and today he will
tell you about what

00:02:32.930 --> 00:02:36.530
we might expect from the field
of robotics and from robots

00:02:36.530 --> 00:02:37.550
into the future.

00:02:45.010 --> 00:02:46.810
So I will share with
you just a few words

00:02:46.810 --> 00:02:50.140
about CSAIL, the organization
we all belong to,

00:02:50.140 --> 00:02:52.520
then we will hear
from the panelists.

00:02:52.520 --> 00:02:55.890
And at the end of
their brief speeches,

00:02:55.890 --> 00:02:59.650
we will open the floor
for general questions.

00:02:59.650 --> 00:03:01.230
So first of about CSAIL.

00:03:01.230 --> 00:03:04.020
We have over 100
senior researchers.

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:06.630
That means faculty and
principle investigators.

00:03:06.630 --> 00:03:08.940
And we have over 500 students.

00:03:08.940 --> 00:03:11.280
That means postdocs,
graduate students,

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:13.560
and undergraduate researchers.

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:16.230
And we are the largest
interdisciplinary research

00:03:16.230 --> 00:03:18.450
laboratory at MIT.

00:03:18.450 --> 00:03:22.360
We were founded 50 years
ago as Project Math,

00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:25.060
and since then,
our laboratory has

00:03:25.060 --> 00:03:29.520
played a very important role in
the technological revolution,

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:34.200
starting with the early days
of time-sharing computers

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:37.710
to the birth of data encryption.

00:03:37.710 --> 00:03:40.860
Our objective is to
teach our students

00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:45.750
to become world-class computer
scientists, to be big thinkers,

00:03:45.750 --> 00:03:48.540
and to become
constructive innovators,

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:51.810
that we take the field of
computing to the next level

00:03:51.810 --> 00:03:54.175
and also take on
some of the world's

00:03:54.175 --> 00:03:56.850
most pressing challenges.

00:03:56.850 --> 00:04:00.540
Our researchers include
robotic researchers like myself

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:04.230
as well as experts in
all fields of computing--

00:04:04.230 --> 00:04:08.160
security, mobile,
wireless, robotics,

00:04:08.160 --> 00:04:13.800
artificial intelligence,
databases, theory.

00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:16.649
You name it, we have
experts in the field.

00:04:16.649 --> 00:04:20.850
And because of this
diverse range of interests,

00:04:20.850 --> 00:04:24.000
we are very excited
and very well equipped

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:27.480
to take on interdisciplinary
challenges.

00:04:27.480 --> 00:04:29.940
And all together,
we imagined a world

00:04:29.940 --> 00:04:35.810
where computing can turn the
world into a better place.

00:04:35.810 --> 00:04:38.640
We imagine a world
where physicians

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:42.570
can use the most advanced
knowledge on genomics

00:04:42.570 --> 00:04:47.100
to engineer better treatments
that are individualized

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:48.870
for patients.

00:04:48.870 --> 00:04:53.310
We imagine a world where
data can help us all

00:04:53.310 --> 00:04:55.620
make better decisions
by predicting

00:04:55.620 --> 00:04:58.740
the consequences
of our decisions

00:04:58.740 --> 00:05:06.210
and where the most recent
advances in encryption

00:05:06.210 --> 00:05:09.900
research, for example,
and differential privacy

00:05:09.900 --> 00:05:12.390
and homomorphic
encryption put people

00:05:12.390 --> 00:05:14.860
in charge of their own data.

00:05:14.860 --> 00:05:17.010
We also imagine a
world where robots

00:05:17.010 --> 00:05:20.580
will be as common as
smartphones and cars

00:05:20.580 --> 00:05:23.570
and where robots
can do everything,

00:05:23.570 --> 00:05:27.120
from helping with search
and rescue activities

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:30.510
to folding your laundry,
which is my least

00:05:30.510 --> 00:05:32.580
favorite chore at home.

00:05:32.580 --> 00:05:34.560
So we really believe
that computing

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:39.630
has a lot to offer in
the way our world will

00:05:39.630 --> 00:05:42.180
be shaping the future.

00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:44.970
And with this, I would
like to stop and turn it

00:05:44.970 --> 00:05:47.700
over to Tim Berners-Lee,
who will talk to us

00:05:47.700 --> 00:05:49.310
about the web we want.

00:05:49.310 --> 00:05:49.810
Tim?

00:05:59.700 --> 00:06:00.670
Thanks.

00:06:00.670 --> 00:06:07.930
So it's great to be here.

00:06:07.930 --> 00:06:09.730
Thank you for coming.

00:06:09.730 --> 00:06:11.510
If you have a--

00:06:11.510 --> 00:06:15.680
put your hand up in the seat
next you, if you have a seat?

00:06:15.680 --> 00:06:20.050
So the awesome seats over
here, if you need them.

00:06:20.050 --> 00:06:22.290
So thank you all for coming.

00:06:22.290 --> 00:06:23.590
Great to be here.

00:06:23.590 --> 00:06:25.980
Thank you back at
South by Southwest.

00:06:25.980 --> 00:06:30.320
It's 25 years ago
that I originally

00:06:30.320 --> 00:06:33.790
wrote a memo proposing the
world wide web project.

00:06:33.790 --> 00:06:39.760
And so-- I want to just check
all of the other [INAUDIBLE]..

00:06:39.760 --> 00:06:50.140
So we've got three the
world wide web project

00:06:50.140 --> 00:06:51.970
I wrote a memo about.

00:06:56.940 --> 00:06:59.520
That was 25 years ago.

00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:02.750
I published a memo, which
has got these scribblings

00:07:02.750 --> 00:07:06.200
on the front about
reorganizing information.

00:07:06.200 --> 00:07:07.750
And for the first
three years, it

00:07:07.750 --> 00:07:09.355
was actually quite difficult
to persuade everybody

00:07:09.355 --> 00:07:10.640
that it would be a good idea.

00:07:10.640 --> 00:07:13.280
It wouldn't be too complicated,
and that they should give out

00:07:13.280 --> 00:07:16.230
their own proprietary
system to open it up,

00:07:16.230 --> 00:07:17.780
the psychological
thing of getting

00:07:17.780 --> 00:07:20.060
open about putting some
of the things on the web.

00:07:20.060 --> 00:07:23.580
But there were
people who got it.

00:07:23.580 --> 00:07:27.355
And just like now when you look
around, South by Southwest,

00:07:27.355 --> 00:07:29.480
you see a lot of people
with a twinkle in their eye

00:07:29.480 --> 00:07:31.380
because they get the idea of
how the world means everything

00:07:31.380 --> 00:07:32.400
in the media.

00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:35.230
25 years, I want to ask
what's the web we want.

00:07:35.230 --> 00:07:39.050
So step back very easy for us
to use the web every moment.

00:07:39.050 --> 00:07:41.940
It's only when you're
under the Mubarak regime

00:07:41.940 --> 00:07:44.870
in Egypt turns the web off or
other things happen on the web

00:07:44.870 --> 00:07:48.440
that you realize that
actually, it's not just

00:07:48.440 --> 00:07:49.730
like the air we breathe.

00:07:49.730 --> 00:07:52.580
You actually have to
step back and think

00:07:52.580 --> 00:07:55.985
about what is behind it and
think about whether it's always

00:07:55.985 --> 00:07:57.985
going to be there and
think about the properties

00:07:57.985 --> 00:07:58.820
that it can.

00:07:58.820 --> 00:08:02.010
But it got us this far now we're
going to continue to happen.

00:08:02.010 --> 00:08:05.700
There are many threats
to rights from the web.

00:08:05.700 --> 00:08:07.200
Recently people
have started to talk

00:08:07.200 --> 00:08:08.940
about human rights on the web.

00:08:08.940 --> 00:08:12.980
Many human rights were
established like the pre web

00:08:12.980 --> 00:08:16.580
builders work, from
what you call and you

00:08:16.580 --> 00:08:19.160
talk about the
Magna Carta in 1215,

00:08:19.160 --> 00:08:21.770
and we talk about the
US Bill of Rights.

00:08:21.770 --> 00:08:23.945
If you're brought
up in South Africa,

00:08:23.945 --> 00:08:26.320
then you're probably very
proud of your new constitution.

00:08:26.320 --> 00:08:28.930
It's put together with a
very good bill of rights,

00:08:28.930 --> 00:08:30.680
but, hopefully, to the
right, none of them

00:08:30.680 --> 00:08:32.850
mention the internet.

00:08:32.850 --> 00:08:34.669
And to some extent,
we can just demand

00:08:34.669 --> 00:08:40.530
those rights we have shouldn't
exist on the internet.

00:08:40.530 --> 00:08:43.100
But in fact, it turns out
that when you go from there,

00:08:43.100 --> 00:08:45.720
then it turns out that
some of the things that you

00:08:45.720 --> 00:08:49.040
wanted like the ability
to connect to anyone--

00:08:49.040 --> 00:08:49.580
[INAUDIBLE]

00:08:49.580 --> 00:08:51.310
Yeah, but that's not really.

00:08:51.310 --> 00:08:53.750
That needs to be called
out because somebody

00:08:53.750 --> 00:08:56.000
could tell you, yes, we're
giving you connectivity.

00:08:56.000 --> 00:08:59.230
You couldn't get these people,
connect to our partners

00:08:59.230 --> 00:09:01.040
through our partner
or companies.

00:09:01.040 --> 00:09:02.660
[INAUDIBLE] and say,
oh, I want to be

00:09:02.660 --> 00:09:06.943
able to connect to any
new upstart company.

00:09:06.943 --> 00:09:08.360
I don't want to
just watch movies,

00:09:08.360 --> 00:09:10.010
I want to watch
movies with anybody

00:09:10.010 --> 00:09:13.350
who has started making a little
startup, making a film company,

00:09:13.350 --> 00:09:13.960
and so on.

00:09:13.960 --> 00:09:18.110
There are a number
of countries who

00:09:18.110 --> 00:09:22.790
are putting internet
regulation legislation, ideas

00:09:22.790 --> 00:09:25.900
about the internet
being a right,

00:09:25.900 --> 00:09:29.810
make sure it's something
obviously the country

00:09:29.810 --> 00:09:31.750
needs you.

00:09:31.750 --> 00:09:34.730
There are various declarations
people got out there,

00:09:34.730 --> 00:09:38.720
the Declaration of
Internet Freedom is one.

00:09:38.720 --> 00:09:40.880
You can check those out.

00:09:40.880 --> 00:09:43.622
You can sign them, and share
with the people somebody

00:09:43.622 --> 00:09:45.080
you might know,
somebody that might

00:09:45.080 --> 00:09:50.970
be hanging around the South
by Southwest who've signed

00:09:50.970 --> 00:09:54.580
that declaration of freedom.

00:09:54.580 --> 00:09:58.450
So what do we mean when we talk
about rights on the internet?

00:09:58.450 --> 00:10:02.100
Well, of course, we're talking
about those thieves or things

00:10:02.100 --> 00:10:04.830
like expressions,
free expressions.

00:10:04.830 --> 00:10:08.790
That means I don't
want to be censored.

00:10:08.790 --> 00:10:11.970
I will need to be able
to get to what I want.

00:10:11.970 --> 00:10:15.120
And also, as a person I
need to put myself out

00:10:15.120 --> 00:10:18.750
there so that anybody else
can see it in any country.

00:10:18.750 --> 00:10:22.028
The web blew up as a
non-national thing.

00:10:22.028 --> 00:10:23.820
At this point, it's an
international thing.

00:10:23.820 --> 00:10:25.780
International sounds like
nations getting together.

00:10:25.780 --> 00:10:26.370
No.

00:10:26.370 --> 00:10:28.185
Web grew up
irrelevant of nations.

00:10:28.185 --> 00:10:30.060
It's ended up, the
internet was connected up,

00:10:30.060 --> 00:10:34.070
and the time and the server
when you connect the web browser

00:10:34.070 --> 00:10:38.160
to the web server, they don't
care about national boundaries.

00:10:38.160 --> 00:10:40.590
That has had some really
sweet side effects in a way,

00:10:40.590 --> 00:10:43.290
but its international
community has grown up.

00:10:43.290 --> 00:10:49.362
Censorship blocks
really messes that up.

00:10:49.362 --> 00:10:50.820
It doesn't happen
in every country.

00:10:50.820 --> 00:10:52.960
Happens in different areas.

00:10:52.960 --> 00:10:55.490
We have to keep track
of that in the future,

00:10:55.490 --> 00:10:59.010
but very important to be
aware where it's happening.

00:10:59.010 --> 00:10:59.580
Access.

00:10:59.580 --> 00:11:02.290
Just ability to access the web.

00:11:04.910 --> 00:11:06.690
So about now, the
number of people

00:11:06.690 --> 00:11:10.200
who got internet, could
access the web technically

00:11:10.200 --> 00:11:13.960
is around 40%.

00:11:13.960 --> 00:11:17.165
So that is great for the 40%.

00:11:17.165 --> 00:11:19.790
And the web internet is becoming
more and more powerful in lots

00:11:19.790 --> 00:11:20.970
of ways, every moment.

00:11:20.970 --> 00:11:22.950
And this component is
more and more cool,

00:11:22.950 --> 00:11:24.990
and it's more and more
empowering for people

00:11:24.990 --> 00:11:28.210
to do their own medicine,
have their own [INAUDIBLE]

00:11:28.210 --> 00:11:32.778
later, in another session
you'll be excited about.

00:11:32.778 --> 00:11:34.320
All these new things
you've done that

00:11:34.320 --> 00:11:35.987
make it more and more
powerful and makes

00:11:35.987 --> 00:11:37.850
the gap between the
40% that do have

00:11:37.850 --> 00:11:40.650
and the 60% that don't
have it even bigger.

00:11:40.650 --> 00:11:45.720
Obviously, for people who don't,
many things get in the way.

00:11:45.720 --> 00:11:48.120
Sometimes it's poverty,
sometimes it's illiteracy.

00:11:48.120 --> 00:11:55.910
Some obviously, are
grouped for mobile.

00:11:55.910 --> 00:11:59.500
In Saharan Africa, in developing
countries, in general,

00:11:59.500 --> 00:12:02.310
it's mobile, the mobile
web, which is taking over.

00:12:02.310 --> 00:12:08.490
Critical things are the
cost of the gadget that

00:12:08.490 --> 00:12:12.950
can do web and the
cost of the beta plans,

00:12:12.950 --> 00:12:15.940
not necessarily broadband,
but some sort of broadband,

00:12:15.940 --> 00:12:20.610
something that will let me do
email, be part of the browsing

00:12:20.610 --> 00:12:23.310
the websites, see how they
do information society

00:12:23.310 --> 00:12:24.870
and recreate it myself.

00:12:24.870 --> 00:12:29.040
I want our people just not just
to go and see the extent of we

00:12:29.040 --> 00:12:32.400
put together by a videographer,
then get on, make it themselves

00:12:32.400 --> 00:12:34.830
in their own language so
you see more and more stuff

00:12:34.830 --> 00:12:36.290
in foreign language.

00:12:36.290 --> 00:12:43.260
So one of the properties of
the web is that it's open.

00:12:43.260 --> 00:12:46.020
That means lots the things.

00:12:46.020 --> 00:12:47.840
Open means lots of things.

00:12:47.840 --> 00:12:50.010
Teach people part how
to build some standards.

00:12:50.010 --> 00:12:52.920
Obviously, many of
you may have worked

00:12:52.920 --> 00:12:56.790
on building web standards
following the latest

00:12:56.790 --> 00:13:01.194
versions of HTML5, the APIs.

00:13:01.194 --> 00:13:04.930
And so keeping those open,
keeping it royalty free.

00:13:04.930 --> 00:13:07.530
A really crucial
point, the place

00:13:07.530 --> 00:13:10.270
I worked for in
Switzerland, CERN,

00:13:10.270 --> 00:13:12.540
that physics lab
are taking months.

00:13:12.540 --> 00:13:14.753
We persuade them
to actually provide

00:13:14.753 --> 00:13:16.170
a document, because
you know what,

00:13:16.170 --> 00:13:18.960
CERN is not going to charge
a lot for the web technology.

00:13:18.960 --> 00:13:21.360
That was a really
crucial point of uptake.

00:13:21.360 --> 00:13:24.480
Since then, it was the point
in the consortium's history

00:13:24.480 --> 00:13:26.955
where we realized we had to
get all consortium members who

00:13:26.955 --> 00:13:28.080
are involved in what we do.

00:13:28.080 --> 00:13:30.300
They also, when we've
come to this fact,

00:13:30.300 --> 00:13:32.577
we aren't going to try to
get royalties for people

00:13:32.577 --> 00:13:33.660
who have implemented them.

00:13:33.660 --> 00:13:35.150
That's the main part of it.

00:13:35.150 --> 00:13:39.000
Now, the corporate part
of being a open support

00:13:39.000 --> 00:13:42.990
is the neutrality of media.

00:13:42.990 --> 00:13:45.580
Censorship is when it's block.

00:13:45.580 --> 00:13:49.450
Net neutrality, it's not
just not being blocked

00:13:49.450 --> 00:13:51.750
or parliament neutrality,
many challenges,

00:13:51.750 --> 00:13:54.480
but you don't mess with it.

00:13:54.480 --> 00:14:00.180
The ISPs, for example,
the famous one in Holland

00:14:00.180 --> 00:14:03.240
that slowed down Skype
because they noticed

00:14:03.240 --> 00:14:07.430
the Skype was starting to eat
into their telephone revenue,

00:14:07.430 --> 00:14:08.680
created that.

00:14:08.680 --> 00:14:10.750
Turned that around
pretty quickly.

00:14:10.750 --> 00:14:13.030
In just legislation,
you can't do that.

00:14:13.030 --> 00:14:17.970
In America, the convention is
that internet service providers

00:14:17.970 --> 00:14:20.550
provide you with connecting
to everyone you want,

00:14:20.550 --> 00:14:22.340
and they don't slow
down packets go

00:14:22.340 --> 00:14:25.410
into their commercial
enemies, but they're always

00:14:25.410 --> 00:14:27.220
under such tremendous
commercial pressure

00:14:27.220 --> 00:14:30.090
to do so because they can
make such a mint if they

00:14:30.090 --> 00:14:33.550
can actually get a stranglehold
on you as a consumer

00:14:33.550 --> 00:14:35.970
and just connect to
who you connect to.

00:14:35.970 --> 00:14:40.750
So this is the Central
American illustrating

00:14:40.750 --> 00:14:42.510
the picture of the
happy ion couple

00:14:42.510 --> 00:14:45.930
underneath all the people
or all the demons trying

00:14:45.930 --> 00:14:52.260
to get up the workings of these
net and use it, some of you

00:14:52.260 --> 00:14:53.520
are on there.

00:14:53.520 --> 00:15:00.170
So many times it's
important, many think,

00:15:00.170 --> 00:15:03.262
I think it's important
for the commercial world,

00:15:03.262 --> 00:15:04.720
for an open market,
of course, it's

00:15:04.720 --> 00:15:06.512
important for the
marketers, it's important

00:15:06.512 --> 00:15:08.900
that you can go to any
physical site you like.

00:15:08.900 --> 00:15:12.020
It's also important for
innovation, the fact

00:15:12.020 --> 00:15:20.240
that you can build a new website
and very quickly one person

00:15:20.240 --> 00:15:24.020
providing the program, two
people promote this program.

00:15:24.020 --> 00:15:26.300
They can send their
package through the cloud

00:15:26.300 --> 00:15:27.660
or the internet.

00:15:27.660 --> 00:15:30.110
By the way, we're
using the cloud diagram

00:15:30.110 --> 00:15:33.830
for the internet on before the
cloud became proudly into our--

00:15:33.830 --> 00:15:34.940
So why you care?

00:15:34.940 --> 00:15:38.330
Because you don't
care what's inside it.

00:15:38.330 --> 00:15:41.450
You can write a program,
she can write program,

00:15:41.450 --> 00:15:46.880
they can communicate, and a new
innovative protocol is born.

00:15:46.880 --> 00:15:48.850
New innovative system
is running over the web,

00:15:48.850 --> 00:15:52.700
a new social media is spreading
across the web, for example.

00:15:52.700 --> 00:15:55.960
That's really important
for innovation,

00:15:55.960 --> 00:15:58.700
so that's why see that
happen in the next 25 years.

00:15:58.700 --> 00:16:01.850
All this, of course, thank
you all for coming here.

00:16:01.850 --> 00:16:07.510
We know you had also, to go
where you had streaming live,

00:16:07.510 --> 00:16:11.420
and the obvious moment,
this is last year,

00:16:11.420 --> 00:16:14.180
there's been a lot
of focus on privacy.

00:16:14.180 --> 00:16:16.400
It's a shame that it
took us some revelations

00:16:16.400 --> 00:16:18.650
to get us all thinking
about the internet.

00:16:18.650 --> 00:16:21.410
Obviously, we have to
think about to what extent

00:16:21.410 --> 00:16:25.660
we give the agencies that's
supposed to protect us,

00:16:25.660 --> 00:16:29.090
the ability to sample the
internet, spy on the internet,

00:16:29.090 --> 00:16:31.590
how I think the really
interesting question is

00:16:31.590 --> 00:16:39.020
how we build systems that
will hold them accountable.

00:16:39.020 --> 00:16:43.370
Checks and balances traditional
in the Madisonian government.

00:16:43.370 --> 00:16:45.650
Checks and balance is equally
built like they've never

00:16:45.650 --> 00:16:46.400
been built before.

00:16:46.400 --> 00:16:49.910
We need very strong agencies
accountable to the public who

00:16:49.910 --> 00:16:54.350
have the power to go in and
investigate the investigators,

00:16:54.350 --> 00:16:56.270
and we don't know how
to build that yet.

00:16:56.270 --> 00:16:59.420
When it doesn't happen,
it's really important

00:16:59.420 --> 00:17:01.250
that we can protest.

00:17:01.250 --> 00:17:04.449
We've got our website
can go as Wiki--

00:17:04.449 --> 00:17:06.130
actually, went
back, not the other.

00:17:09.660 --> 00:17:12.190
So that was when
Wikipedia went back

00:17:12.190 --> 00:17:15.980
is when the SOPA legislation
was going to go through.

00:17:15.980 --> 00:17:19.010
Also, people went
out into the streets

00:17:19.010 --> 00:17:24.260
protesting SOPA in the US,
protesting ACTA in Europe.

00:17:24.260 --> 00:17:26.190
ACTA was an agreement
which was going

00:17:26.190 --> 00:17:31.040
to give too much power
to copyright holders

00:17:31.040 --> 00:17:34.080
and take away freedoms
from individuals.

00:17:34.080 --> 00:17:36.905
So this sort of stuff
is important to be

00:17:36.905 --> 00:17:37.790
able to do that.

00:17:37.790 --> 00:17:39.290
There are lots the
things you have

00:17:39.290 --> 00:17:41.750
to be able to protest
about, lots of things

00:17:41.750 --> 00:17:43.940
when actually
individual voices have

00:17:43.940 --> 00:17:51.500
to be added up so that this
thing is kept open and free.

00:17:51.500 --> 00:17:54.740
Those of you who
were here last year,

00:17:54.740 --> 00:17:57.950
we were talking about
Aaron Swartz's life

00:17:57.950 --> 00:18:02.580
and how he was a huge
part of software so far.

00:18:02.580 --> 00:18:05.480
Let's keep doing, in
his memory keep doing

00:18:05.480 --> 00:18:07.790
the things what would Aaron do.

00:18:07.790 --> 00:18:15.860
So those are some of the
things which I think.

00:18:15.860 --> 00:18:19.160
Now, back on my
agenda for the web

00:18:19.160 --> 00:18:24.030
we want, so there is a web we
want node called Web at 25.

00:18:27.610 --> 00:18:29.510
Well, web 25 that's
going to be live.

00:18:29.510 --> 00:18:34.580
On the top of March, which is
another lecture of the web.

00:18:34.580 --> 00:18:36.980
I want you all to think
about what sort of world web

00:18:36.980 --> 00:18:37.760
you want.

00:18:37.760 --> 00:18:40.200
I want you to not
just think about it.

00:18:40.200 --> 00:18:42.540
I want you when
necessary to take action.

00:18:42.540 --> 00:18:43.610
Make sure.

00:18:43.610 --> 00:18:46.260
I have faith that by
the end of the year,

00:18:46.260 --> 00:18:48.625
we know what legislation
is properly enacted,

00:18:48.625 --> 00:18:49.690
in which countries.

00:18:49.690 --> 00:18:52.460
In all of your countries,
what sort of pressure

00:18:52.460 --> 00:18:55.820
is going to be put on
the companies necessary.

00:18:55.820 --> 00:19:00.330
What sort of spirit do
we want behind the web

00:19:00.330 --> 00:19:03.010
that we're going to have
for the next 25 years.

00:19:03.010 --> 00:19:04.761
Thank you very
much for your time.

00:19:13.850 --> 00:19:16.630
So next, I would
like to turn it over

00:19:16.630 --> 00:19:20.380
to Andrew Lo, who will talk to
us about big data, big brother,

00:19:20.380 --> 00:19:21.630
and the financial crisis.

00:19:21.630 --> 00:19:22.130
Andrew.

00:19:22.130 --> 00:19:23.070
Thank you.

00:19:27.800 --> 00:19:30.050
Also, while we get
the slides set up,

00:19:30.050 --> 00:19:31.850
I want to start by
thanking Daniela

00:19:31.850 --> 00:19:34.690
Rus and my other
colleagues at CSAIL

00:19:34.690 --> 00:19:37.000
and the organizers
of South by Southwest

00:19:37.000 --> 00:19:41.863
for inviting me to participate
in this great panel.

00:19:41.863 --> 00:19:43.780
I have to admit that as
a financial economist,

00:19:43.780 --> 00:19:46.900
I feel a little bit out
of water and intimidated

00:19:46.900 --> 00:19:49.330
because if you look at the
composition of the panel,

00:19:49.330 --> 00:19:52.660
we've got two of the leading
roboticists of our generation

00:19:52.660 --> 00:19:55.240
and the inventor of
the internet and me.

00:19:55.240 --> 00:19:58.360
So who ordered that?

00:19:58.360 --> 00:20:00.640
But when I told a
friend of mine that I

00:20:00.640 --> 00:20:03.310
was coming to South by
Southwest to be on this panel,

00:20:03.310 --> 00:20:06.700
he was very jealous and
said, why are you going?

00:20:06.700 --> 00:20:10.210
Isn't South by Southwest all
about disruptive innovation?

00:20:10.210 --> 00:20:11.830
And I have to say
that absolutely,

00:20:11.830 --> 00:20:13.360
and financial
economists belong here

00:20:13.360 --> 00:20:16.660
because at least the
first pact we've got down.

00:20:16.660 --> 00:20:20.250
Finance has been
incredibly disruptive.

00:20:20.250 --> 00:20:23.870
And so what I want to talk about
is, what do we do about it.

00:20:23.870 --> 00:20:25.930
How do we deal
with the challenges

00:20:25.930 --> 00:20:28.480
of a financial crisis,
and particularly

00:20:28.480 --> 00:20:33.250
big data and the privacy
issues that Tim mentioned.

00:20:33.250 --> 00:20:36.520
So I'd like to start by
talking a bit about what

00:20:36.520 --> 00:20:40.270
we know of the economy.

00:20:40.270 --> 00:20:42.790
And it turns out that
there's actually quite a lot

00:20:42.790 --> 00:20:46.480
that we know if we actually
take a look at the data

00:20:46.480 --> 00:20:47.597
that we have.

00:20:47.597 --> 00:20:48.430
I can break this up.

00:20:51.490 --> 00:20:55.030
Over the course of the
last several years,

00:20:55.030 --> 00:20:57.430
the economy has been
going up and down,

00:20:57.430 --> 00:21:01.000
but over the last few months, it
looks like things are actually

00:21:01.000 --> 00:21:02.690
looking pretty good.

00:21:02.690 --> 00:21:06.610
So for example, if you take a
look at recent announcements

00:21:06.610 --> 00:21:09.720
by the Bureau of
Economic Analysis--

00:21:09.720 --> 00:21:12.110
I'll actually bring up the
slides in just a minute.

00:21:16.351 --> 00:21:19.273
[INAUDIBLE]

00:21:23.169 --> 00:21:26.580
There we go.

00:21:26.580 --> 00:21:29.505
[INAUDIBLE]

00:21:32.980 --> 00:21:34.990
Yeah, great.

00:21:34.990 --> 00:21:37.760
So what do we know
about the economy?

00:21:37.760 --> 00:21:39.490
Well, as I mentioned,
we've got a lot

00:21:39.490 --> 00:21:43.150
of data coming in, so we know
that inflation is about 1/10

00:21:43.150 --> 00:21:48.000
of a percent; unemployment,
6.6%; GDP growth, 2.4%;

00:21:48.000 --> 00:21:52.630
non-farm payroll, 113,000;
housing spark, 180,000.

00:21:52.630 --> 00:21:55.660
The fed balance sheet
is $2.6 trillion.

00:21:55.660 --> 00:21:59.410
All of these data tell us
where we are in the economy,

00:21:59.410 --> 00:22:01.660
that basically things
are getting better,

00:22:01.660 --> 00:22:04.910
but we've still
got a ways to go.

00:22:04.910 --> 00:22:08.050
However, there is a
huge part of the economy

00:22:08.050 --> 00:22:10.960
that we actually
don't know much about,

00:22:10.960 --> 00:22:14.080
for example, hedge funds
and the shadow banking

00:22:14.080 --> 00:22:19.310
system that were central in
creating the financial crisis.

00:22:19.310 --> 00:22:20.712
What do we know about that?

00:22:20.712 --> 00:22:22.420
Well, let me show you
the data that we've

00:22:22.420 --> 00:22:25.780
collected about hedge funds
and the shadow banking sector.

00:22:30.010 --> 00:22:31.920
This is what we know about it--

00:22:31.920 --> 00:22:33.283
nothing.

00:22:33.283 --> 00:22:35.200
And the reason we don't
know anything about it

00:22:35.200 --> 00:22:38.710
is because there is actually
no legal requirements

00:22:38.710 --> 00:22:42.850
for hedge funds or the
shadow banks to report.

00:22:42.850 --> 00:22:47.180
They're not even actually under
any regulatory jurisdiction.

00:22:47.180 --> 00:22:49.720
And so the old adage
goes, you can't

00:22:49.720 --> 00:22:54.730
manage what you don't measure,
applies to this sector.

00:22:54.730 --> 00:22:57.850
And clearly, we
want to understand

00:22:57.850 --> 00:23:01.810
what these data are telling us
about that part of the system.

00:23:01.810 --> 00:23:04.450
But at the same time, a lot of
these financial institutions

00:23:04.450 --> 00:23:07.630
have argued, well,
we're developing

00:23:07.630 --> 00:23:10.600
proprietary trading
strategies and investments

00:23:10.600 --> 00:23:12.160
for our shareholders.

00:23:12.160 --> 00:23:14.170
And if you believe in
private enterprise,

00:23:14.170 --> 00:23:16.120
you don't want to
force us to disclose.

00:23:16.120 --> 00:23:19.630
After all, you don't
disclose Coca-Cola's formula,

00:23:19.630 --> 00:23:21.130
you want to give
people an incentive

00:23:21.130 --> 00:23:23.080
to develop new innovations.

00:23:23.080 --> 00:23:24.760
So the question is,
is there some kind

00:23:24.760 --> 00:23:30.917
of a compromise between
privacy and total transparency?

00:23:30.917 --> 00:23:33.250
And it's pretty clear that
there are certain things that

00:23:33.250 --> 00:23:34.960
have to be kept private.

00:23:34.960 --> 00:23:38.080
We don't want
total transparency.

00:23:38.080 --> 00:23:40.690
The answer is, yes, there's
actually a compromise,

00:23:40.690 --> 00:23:43.840
and it has to do with
a new technology,

00:23:43.840 --> 00:23:46.150
not so new to computer
science literature,

00:23:46.150 --> 00:23:48.660
but new in its
applications to finance.

00:23:48.660 --> 00:23:52.120
And the technology is known as
secure multi-party computation.

00:23:52.120 --> 00:23:55.330
Let me tell you what it
is with a simple example.

00:23:55.330 --> 00:23:59.170
I've always wondered what
the socioeconomic statuses

00:23:59.170 --> 00:24:02.260
of participants at
South by Southwest.

00:24:02.260 --> 00:24:03.910
So one of the things
I'd like to know

00:24:03.910 --> 00:24:09.110
is what the average salary
is of people in this room.

00:24:09.110 --> 00:24:10.690
So let me start
by asking, anybody

00:24:10.690 --> 00:24:12.940
you want to tell me what
their average salary is so we

00:24:12.940 --> 00:24:16.210
can start computing an average?

00:24:16.210 --> 00:24:17.680
Not so much.

00:24:17.680 --> 00:24:20.985
Well, we obviously
acknowledge that salaries

00:24:20.985 --> 00:24:22.750
are a quite confidential thing.

00:24:22.750 --> 00:24:25.370
So let me tell you what
my annual salary is

00:24:25.370 --> 00:24:27.280
so we can start
trying to compute

00:24:27.280 --> 00:24:29.500
the average in this room.

00:24:29.500 --> 00:24:32.152
But I'm not going to tell you
what my salary is by itself.

00:24:32.152 --> 00:24:33.610
What I'm going to
do is to tell you

00:24:33.610 --> 00:24:37.750
what my annual salary is
plus a random number that I

00:24:37.750 --> 00:24:41.210
choose and add to my salary.

00:24:41.210 --> 00:24:47.320
So my salary plus the
random number is $15.

00:24:47.320 --> 00:24:49.590
So hopefully, I added a
negative number to my salary.

00:24:52.180 --> 00:24:54.660
So I'm happy to share
that with you, $15.

00:24:54.660 --> 00:24:56.850
And I'm going to give that
number to somebody else

00:24:56.850 --> 00:24:58.900
in the audience, say, Alice.

00:24:58.900 --> 00:25:02.670
And I'm going to ask Alice
to take that number, $15,

00:25:02.670 --> 00:25:06.270
and add to that her salary
plus her random number

00:25:06.270 --> 00:25:09.930
that only she knows
and she chooses.

00:25:09.930 --> 00:25:15.150
And so Alice does that, and
the sum is maybe $37,000.

00:25:15.150 --> 00:25:16.830
And Alice takes that
number and gives it

00:25:16.830 --> 00:25:21.240
to somebody else who adds
his number and his salary

00:25:21.240 --> 00:25:22.775
and passes it along
to somebody else

00:25:22.775 --> 00:25:24.150
and to somebody
else and somebody

00:25:24.150 --> 00:25:27.780
else until the last person in
the room, Bob, gets the number.

00:25:27.780 --> 00:25:33.430
And Bob adds to it his
salary and his random number.

00:25:33.430 --> 00:25:37.320
And at that point, the number
turns out to be $15 billion,

00:25:37.320 --> 00:25:40.230
and Bob gives it back to me.

00:25:40.230 --> 00:25:44.520
I take that number, $15 billion,
and I subtract from that

00:25:44.520 --> 00:25:46.590
my random number.

00:25:46.590 --> 00:25:47.700
I give it to Alice.

00:25:47.700 --> 00:25:50.220
She subtracts her random number.

00:25:50.220 --> 00:25:52.410
She passes it and gives
it to the next person who

00:25:52.410 --> 00:25:56.700
subtracts her random number and
so on and so forth until Bob

00:25:56.700 --> 00:25:59.370
subtract his random number.

00:25:59.370 --> 00:26:03.540
And at that point, when Bob
subtracts his random number,

00:26:03.540 --> 00:26:07.785
what Bob has is the sum of
everybody's salary, which,

00:26:07.785 --> 00:26:09.660
when you divide by the
total number of people

00:26:09.660 --> 00:26:13.440
in the room, which everybody can
see, and is public information,

00:26:13.440 --> 00:26:19.890
yields the average salary
of everybody in the room.

00:26:19.890 --> 00:26:24.840
Nowhere did anybody have to
reveal his or her salary,

00:26:24.840 --> 00:26:28.080
but at the end of the day,
we actually have the average.

00:26:28.080 --> 00:26:30.600
Now, of course, this algorithm
is a pretty simple one

00:26:30.600 --> 00:26:32.820
in the sense that if I
got together with Alice,

00:26:32.820 --> 00:26:34.830
I could actually
cheat and figure out

00:26:34.830 --> 00:26:37.500
what Bob's salary
is, but there are

00:26:37.500 --> 00:26:40.230
more sophisticated
algorithms that

00:26:40.230 --> 00:26:45.600
allow no cheating to go on
and yet permit us to calculate

00:26:45.600 --> 00:26:49.560
numbers like average salaries
or average loan portfolios

00:26:49.560 --> 00:26:53.310
or average concentration
indexes of credit default swaps,

00:26:53.310 --> 00:26:56.670
things that will tell us what
the systemic exposures are

00:26:56.670 --> 00:27:00.960
for the aggregate economy
without jeopardizing privacy.

00:27:00.960 --> 00:27:02.760
So let me conclude
by pointing out

00:27:02.760 --> 00:27:05.850
that with the right technology,
transparency and privacy can

00:27:05.850 --> 00:27:06.870
both be achieved.

00:27:06.870 --> 00:27:09.180
You don't have to give
it up as long as you

00:27:09.180 --> 00:27:11.460
have the wherewithal
and the right tools

00:27:11.460 --> 00:27:13.510
to be able to deal with
these kind of challenges.

00:27:13.510 --> 00:27:14.154
Thank you.

00:27:14.154 --> 00:27:15.636
Thank you, Andrew.

00:27:21.080 --> 00:27:24.010
Next, I'll turn it over
to Russ Tedrake, who

00:27:24.010 --> 00:27:26.540
will tell us why the
robots are coming here,

00:27:26.540 --> 00:27:29.150
and how they're coming here.

00:27:29.150 --> 00:27:29.650
Yeah.

00:27:29.650 --> 00:27:30.150
Hi there.

00:27:30.150 --> 00:27:33.160
So it's sort of a cheesy
title, The Robot Are Coming.

00:27:33.160 --> 00:27:34.660
I had to think a
lot about whether I

00:27:34.660 --> 00:27:36.035
was going to use
the title, but I

00:27:36.035 --> 00:27:38.620
feel like there's never been a
time before where I would have

00:27:38.620 --> 00:27:40.870
felt right using the
title, and there may never

00:27:40.870 --> 00:27:42.910
be a time again
where I'll feel right

00:27:42.910 --> 00:27:44.080
using such a cheesy title.

00:27:44.080 --> 00:27:45.970
But let me tell you why.

00:27:45.970 --> 00:27:47.590
Robots are trending
in such a way

00:27:47.590 --> 00:27:50.638
right now, just in
a massive proportion

00:27:50.638 --> 00:27:52.930
that this felt like the one
time where I could get away

00:27:52.930 --> 00:27:53.918
maybe using the title.

00:27:53.918 --> 00:27:55.460
And maybe it's not
even their coming,

00:27:55.460 --> 00:27:56.830
but they're actually here.

00:27:56.830 --> 00:28:00.280
And I want to make sure you guys
know the extent to which they

00:28:00.280 --> 00:28:00.980
are trending.

00:28:00.980 --> 00:28:04.020
And it happened with
a lot of progress

00:28:04.020 --> 00:28:06.490
in academia and industry.

00:28:06.490 --> 00:28:10.360
And then it got
quickly spurred on

00:28:10.360 --> 00:28:13.390
by this relatively
massive investment

00:28:13.390 --> 00:28:14.450
first by the government.

00:28:14.450 --> 00:28:16.660
So there's a National
Robotics Initiative

00:28:16.660 --> 00:28:19.960
that happened three years
ago now where the robotics

00:28:19.960 --> 00:28:22.360
community got together,
formed a robotics caucus,

00:28:22.360 --> 00:28:26.050
and the Congress made
robotics a national agenda

00:28:26.050 --> 00:28:31.750
and produced research money for
academic research in robotics.

00:28:31.750 --> 00:28:34.630
There's another big huge
another $80 million or so

00:28:34.630 --> 00:28:37.030
coming from DARPA that
was injected into building

00:28:37.030 --> 00:28:38.530
robotic hardware in the US.

00:28:38.530 --> 00:28:41.590
So you're just seeing
many, many more robots

00:28:41.590 --> 00:28:44.800
moving around, walking
around in the United States.

00:28:44.800 --> 00:28:46.900
It's a worldwide phenomenon.

00:28:46.900 --> 00:28:50.260
All over, the European Union has
massive funding for robotics.

00:28:50.260 --> 00:28:54.970
Japan and Korea have
been leaders in robotics,

00:28:54.970 --> 00:28:58.270
even at MIT where
we've been spending

00:28:58.270 --> 00:29:02.170
our precious few junior
faculty slots filling in

00:29:02.170 --> 00:29:05.380
with roboticists because we
know that robotics is hot.

00:29:05.380 --> 00:29:07.660
It's hot now.

00:29:07.660 --> 00:29:10.300
Google just shocked
the world in December

00:29:10.300 --> 00:29:14.860
saying that they purchased eight
of the big robotics companies

00:29:14.860 --> 00:29:16.955
in the last six months.

00:29:16.955 --> 00:29:18.080
So why did all this happen?

00:29:18.080 --> 00:29:20.980
Why is it all so much energy?

00:29:20.980 --> 00:29:25.447
Because in the last five or
six years, robots got awesome.

00:29:25.447 --> 00:29:27.030
You must have seen
this before, right?

00:29:27.030 --> 00:29:30.730
So this Wild Katherine
Boston Dynamics.

00:29:30.730 --> 00:29:34.390
This is UADs that are flying
around individually doing

00:29:34.390 --> 00:29:38.140
amazing things, but
collectively doing amazing swarm

00:29:38.140 --> 00:29:41.350
computations that scale.

00:29:41.350 --> 00:29:43.660
That's at UPenn.

00:29:43.660 --> 00:29:45.190
This is an airplane.

00:29:45.190 --> 00:29:47.080
Airplanes don't normally
land on a perch.

00:29:47.080 --> 00:29:49.720
This airplane would
developed at CSAIL.

00:29:49.720 --> 00:29:52.480
It can stall its wings
and like a bird and land

00:29:52.480 --> 00:29:54.430
on a perch like a bird.

00:29:54.430 --> 00:29:56.380
Maybe it will land
on power lines

00:29:56.380 --> 00:29:59.440
to triple charge its
batteries then keep flying.

00:29:59.440 --> 00:30:01.220
And in fact, it's so
robust that now we

00:30:01.220 --> 00:30:03.640
have Joe just throwing
airplanes at wires all day long.

00:30:07.680 --> 00:30:09.740
Manipulation
capabilities of robots,

00:30:09.740 --> 00:30:11.610
the hardware technology
that we've got now,

00:30:11.610 --> 00:30:13.440
this is from Germany.

00:30:13.440 --> 00:30:16.440
This just showcases the
robotic arm technology

00:30:16.440 --> 00:30:18.810
that is mature, the
robotic hand technology

00:30:18.810 --> 00:30:24.400
able to do relatively advanced
manipulation paths with flair.

00:30:28.140 --> 00:30:30.390
The sensing
capabilities of robotics

00:30:30.390 --> 00:30:32.850
has been just
phenomenal progress.

00:30:32.850 --> 00:30:36.887
This is a connect sensor
that one of John Leonard's

00:30:36.887 --> 00:30:39.220
colleagues, Tom Whelan, is
walking through his apartment

00:30:39.220 --> 00:30:43.660
once with a Microsoft
connect sensor, prime sensor.

00:30:43.660 --> 00:30:45.890
And after just a single
traverse of the apartment,

00:30:45.890 --> 00:30:48.430
look at the kind of
map he can pull out.

00:30:48.430 --> 00:30:50.320
But robots can see now.

00:30:50.320 --> 00:30:52.570
They can't necessarily
understand what they're seeing,

00:30:52.570 --> 00:30:56.370
but the geometric representation
of the world problem

00:30:56.370 --> 00:30:59.040
is on a wave.

00:30:59.040 --> 00:31:02.890
Robots can see the environment.

00:31:02.890 --> 00:31:05.580
And now robots are going out and
taking all these technologies

00:31:05.580 --> 00:31:08.647
and putting them together
and solving hard problems out

00:31:08.647 --> 00:31:10.230
in the real world,
but one of the ones

00:31:10.230 --> 00:31:12.180
that I've been involved
in the last year

00:31:12.180 --> 00:31:14.880
is this project by
DARPA, a competition

00:31:14.880 --> 00:31:17.455
by DARPA, where we
had to field robots.

00:31:17.455 --> 00:31:19.080
They didn't have to
be humanoid robots,

00:31:19.080 --> 00:31:22.320
but used the humanoid robot
that had to go out and handle

00:31:22.320 --> 00:31:23.890
a disaster response scenario.

00:31:23.890 --> 00:31:25.800
So this is the scene
where they put us

00:31:25.800 --> 00:31:26.950
in front of a debris pile.

00:31:26.950 --> 00:31:30.410
We had to clear debris
and get through--

00:31:30.410 --> 00:31:32.863
to imagine the door at
the end of the debris.

00:31:32.863 --> 00:31:35.280
But the point is, we didn't
set this up and pick one video

00:31:35.280 --> 00:31:36.388
and put it on YouTube.

00:31:36.388 --> 00:31:38.430
This is a challenge that
had to work on game day.

00:31:38.430 --> 00:31:40.590
We had one shot
with the real robot.

00:31:40.590 --> 00:31:42.780
The robot we some
human operators that

00:31:42.780 --> 00:31:45.363
were connected to the robot with
a very degraded network link.

00:31:45.363 --> 00:31:47.572
So they were allowed to get
high-level commands where

00:31:47.572 --> 00:31:49.725
they couldn't steer and
control the whole robot,

00:31:49.725 --> 00:31:51.600
and I'll just show you
a really quick summary

00:31:51.600 --> 00:31:53.190
of the kind of things we do.

00:31:53.190 --> 00:31:55.980
I set it up, so I didn't
use too much of my time.

00:31:55.980 --> 00:31:58.290
But the robot is pretty
amazingly capable.

00:31:58.290 --> 00:32:03.945
In fact, this was a good thing
for the bipeds in the world.

00:32:03.945 --> 00:32:05.820
We just had this big
competition in December.

00:32:05.820 --> 00:32:07.430
Everybody said
that the people who

00:32:07.430 --> 00:32:09.570
are bringing bipedal
robots, all the robots

00:32:09.570 --> 00:32:11.862
are just going to fall down
an embarrassing themselves.

00:32:11.862 --> 00:32:13.695
But in fact, the
robots did very well.

00:32:13.695 --> 00:32:14.820
The bipedals did very well.

00:32:14.820 --> 00:32:16.448
This guy happens
to be so wide that.

00:32:16.448 --> 00:32:18.740
I don't know if you remember
Hans and Franz on Saturday

00:32:18.740 --> 00:32:21.580
Night Live.

00:32:21.580 --> 00:32:24.310
The only way we can get through
the door was going sideways.

00:32:24.310 --> 00:32:26.650
It cost is valuable
time, but that was it.

00:32:26.650 --> 00:32:29.682
This is taking up a
drill, turning it on,

00:32:29.682 --> 00:32:31.640
and then you got to cut
of shape out of a wall.

00:32:35.770 --> 00:32:36.900
And these aren't walls.

00:32:36.900 --> 00:32:39.350
These are things that had
to work on competition day,

00:32:39.350 --> 00:32:46.380
so picking up a fire hose
and mating it with a spigot,

00:32:46.380 --> 00:32:47.960
we can do this in the wild now.

00:32:53.310 --> 00:32:53.810
Slowly.

00:32:58.030 --> 00:32:59.740
And DARPA put on
this amazing event

00:32:59.740 --> 00:33:03.760
where they've actually rented
a NASCAR stadium, the Homestead

00:33:03.760 --> 00:33:07.480
Speedway, and we had all the 18
most advanced humanoid robots

00:33:07.480 --> 00:33:08.290
in the world.

00:33:08.290 --> 00:33:10.248
And each of these could
walk down Pit Crew Lane

00:33:10.248 --> 00:33:11.950
and see all these
humanoids and all

00:33:11.950 --> 00:33:13.690
the most stressed
out programmers

00:33:13.690 --> 00:33:16.020
in the world working on
their robots the day before.

00:33:16.020 --> 00:33:19.507
And they put it through
all these competitions.

00:33:19.507 --> 00:33:21.340
All right, so it's easy
to say that and then

00:33:21.340 --> 00:33:23.270
make you think that
robotics is solved,

00:33:23.270 --> 00:33:25.210
but there's lots of
things we can't do yet.

00:33:25.210 --> 00:33:29.440
So the robots today were
moving not very gracefully,

00:33:29.440 --> 00:33:31.180
very slowly.

00:33:31.180 --> 00:33:33.310
We wanted them to
move like ballerinas.

00:33:33.310 --> 00:33:35.980
I would be happy if our
Atlas robot, our big humanoid

00:33:35.980 --> 00:33:37.450
had the manipulation
capabilities

00:33:37.450 --> 00:33:41.560
of a two-year-old or
three-year-old here.

00:33:41.560 --> 00:33:44.260
If I have a factory where I have
a perfectly specified object,

00:33:44.260 --> 00:33:45.020
maybe I'm OK.

00:33:45.020 --> 00:33:48.300
But if I have unknown objects
I haven't seen before,

00:33:48.300 --> 00:33:50.770
it's still a very
hard problem for us.

00:33:50.770 --> 00:33:52.570
Object recognition,
scene understanding,

00:33:52.570 --> 00:33:53.945
high-level decision
making, these

00:33:53.945 --> 00:33:55.660
are all technical
problems that we're

00:33:55.660 --> 00:33:58.420
going to be tackling
in the next few years.

00:33:58.420 --> 00:34:00.130
But there's big
problems with robotics

00:34:00.130 --> 00:34:04.210
too, bigger than these one-off
research problems, the thing

00:34:04.210 --> 00:34:07.720
that's going to get the robots
out of the YouTube world

00:34:07.720 --> 00:34:11.122
and into the real world is
solving problems about robots

00:34:11.122 --> 00:34:12.080
being tipped gradually.

00:34:12.080 --> 00:34:14.307
They fall down, they break.

00:34:14.307 --> 00:34:16.599
It's very depressing when
you work that hard on a robot

00:34:16.599 --> 00:34:18.070
and they break.

00:34:18.070 --> 00:34:19.870
But there's new
fabrication technologies

00:34:19.870 --> 00:34:21.460
out there that, for
instance, Daniela

00:34:21.460 --> 00:34:24.190
is working on that are
making soft robots that

00:34:24.190 --> 00:34:26.860
will be able to recover from--
this is one of Daniela's videos

00:34:26.860 --> 00:34:28.690
of a earthworm
robot that she hits

00:34:28.690 --> 00:34:31.620
with a mallet at all times.

00:34:31.620 --> 00:34:34.120
And if we can figure out how
to not only build these things,

00:34:34.120 --> 00:34:37.659
but control these things to be
as dexterous as the videos I've

00:34:37.659 --> 00:34:39.969
been showing you,
that can totally

00:34:39.969 --> 00:34:42.699
reshape the robotic landscape.

00:34:42.699 --> 00:34:45.230
Robots are too expensive.

00:34:45.230 --> 00:34:47.889
We spent millions of dollars
on that humanoid robot you saw.

00:34:47.889 --> 00:34:49.300
And if I want to send
them out into the world,

00:34:49.300 --> 00:34:51.610
we're going to have to make
them much, much cheaper,

00:34:51.610 --> 00:34:54.190
and soft robotics can
help with that too.

00:34:54.190 --> 00:34:55.679
They're using too much energy.

00:34:55.679 --> 00:34:58.270
The humanoid robot I
showed you actually,

00:34:58.270 --> 00:35:01.420
the reason it had a tether was
because it pulls 15 kilowatts

00:35:01.420 --> 00:35:03.730
off the wall when
it's standing still.

00:35:03.730 --> 00:35:06.220
So if you've seen
the big dog robot,

00:35:06.220 --> 00:35:09.910
the big dog has a 15 horsepower
motor, and it's about 1/8

00:35:09.910 --> 00:35:10.580
of a horse.

00:35:10.580 --> 00:35:12.913
So I guess if you do the math,
it's about 100 times less

00:35:12.913 --> 00:35:14.465
efficient than a horse.

00:35:14.465 --> 00:35:16.090
We're going to have
to solve that if we

00:35:16.090 --> 00:35:17.710
want to get them out there.

00:35:17.710 --> 00:35:19.720
We also have to make
it easier for people

00:35:19.720 --> 00:35:21.260
to work on robots
out in the field

00:35:21.260 --> 00:35:24.400
and give them legislation
to support this community

00:35:24.400 --> 00:35:26.350
and foster the environment.

00:35:26.350 --> 00:35:27.460
But this is a good time.

00:35:27.460 --> 00:35:29.043
This is an amazing
time for you to get

00:35:29.043 --> 00:35:32.380
involved if you're thinking
about a robotics startup.

00:35:32.380 --> 00:35:35.410
There's never been
a better time where

00:35:35.410 --> 00:35:38.500
there's cop sensors out
there, off-the-shelf sensors,

00:35:38.500 --> 00:35:40.810
actuators, even robots.

00:35:40.810 --> 00:35:42.610
The robotics
community has finally

00:35:42.610 --> 00:35:44.350
embraced the open
source movement,

00:35:44.350 --> 00:35:47.470
both for hardware and
software, the maker movement

00:35:47.470 --> 00:35:49.240
has impacted robotics
in a major way.

00:35:49.240 --> 00:35:51.340
You can download plans
for entire humanoids

00:35:51.340 --> 00:35:53.590
now and get involved.

00:35:53.590 --> 00:35:55.840
We just released the
entire planning and control

00:35:55.840 --> 00:35:58.600
infrastructure code base for
that humanoid robot I showed.

00:35:58.600 --> 00:36:01.960
If you're interested in
using that, send me an email.

00:36:01.960 --> 00:36:03.580
I will help you
build this because I

00:36:03.580 --> 00:36:08.160
think this is so important
for robotics to get out there.

00:36:08.160 --> 00:36:10.000
You can downline machine shops.

00:36:10.000 --> 00:36:12.580
I think if you guys can dream
up new consumer applications

00:36:12.580 --> 00:36:15.130
and we can work together,
then we can drive down costs,

00:36:15.130 --> 00:36:17.017
and we can solve
really hard problems.

00:36:17.017 --> 00:36:18.850
And there's never been
a more important time

00:36:18.850 --> 00:36:22.630
for academia to team with
industry and other academics

00:36:22.630 --> 00:36:26.860
to bring in the
next wave of robots.

00:36:26.860 --> 00:36:30.108
I think we're really entering
an exciting era for robotics.

00:36:30.108 --> 00:36:31.602
Thank you, Russ.

00:36:38.574 --> 00:36:41.770
Well, I hope you can
see the enthusiasm

00:36:41.770 --> 00:36:45.490
that we have for the future
and for what computing can

00:36:45.490 --> 00:36:51.160
do to improve our lives and
give us better ways to work,

00:36:51.160 --> 00:36:54.240
to play, and to live.

00:36:54.240 --> 00:36:57.280
And so please join us
in this enthusiasm,

00:36:57.280 --> 00:36:59.460
and please join
us in the Q&amp;A. Let

00:36:59.460 --> 00:37:02.020
me start the Q&amp;A session
by asking each one

00:37:02.020 --> 00:37:04.330
of our panelists one question.

00:37:04.330 --> 00:37:06.830
And I see that there are
microphones in the aisles.

00:37:06.830 --> 00:37:10.190
Please join us with
your questions.

00:37:10.190 --> 00:37:15.010
So Tim, you created this
really exciting view

00:37:15.010 --> 00:37:18.400
for the future of the world
wide web, for the web we want.

00:37:18.400 --> 00:37:25.090
What new technologies might be
needed to get to your dream?

00:37:25.090 --> 00:37:30.040
Well, so the current state
we're at, web developer, HTML5,

00:37:30.040 --> 00:37:32.960
what's different about HTML5
compared to the original web I

00:37:32.960 --> 00:37:37.330
started 25 years ago is
that every web page instead,

00:37:37.330 --> 00:37:38.878
is completely programmable.

00:37:38.878 --> 00:37:40.420
And the power that
we're giving as we

00:37:40.420 --> 00:37:44.860
get more APIs, like the
system APIs to the web page

00:37:44.860 --> 00:37:48.280
means that you can program
your web page to do anything

00:37:48.280 --> 00:37:50.720
the programmer computed with.

00:37:50.720 --> 00:37:54.970
And with real-time
communication, WebRTC,

00:37:54.970 --> 00:37:57.790
you could get two web pages or
two versions of the same web

00:37:57.790 --> 00:38:00.790
page run by different
users talking to each other

00:38:00.790 --> 00:38:03.110
without necessarily
going through the server.

00:38:03.110 --> 00:38:06.220
So you can build a new
distributed application

00:38:06.220 --> 00:38:10.120
using peer-to-peer protocols
across the internet based

00:38:10.120 --> 00:38:12.730
on web app technology.

00:38:12.730 --> 00:38:16.090
So that is happening right now.

00:38:16.090 --> 00:38:19.450
It's going to happen in
browsers like we use [INAUDIBLE]

00:38:19.450 --> 00:38:20.650
really exciting.

00:38:20.650 --> 00:38:24.450
And I imagine that
will cause people

00:38:24.450 --> 00:38:29.020
to build really exciting,
new platforms on top of that.

00:38:29.020 --> 00:38:31.216
So that's an example
that we have from web

00:38:31.216 --> 00:38:34.570
on top of the
internet, web hoopla,

00:38:34.570 --> 00:38:38.505
web app on top of the web and
exciting things on top of that.

00:38:38.505 --> 00:38:39.130
Thank you, Tim.

00:38:39.130 --> 00:38:44.860
So in your view of the web, you
have articulated the importance

00:38:44.860 --> 00:38:48.380
of privacy, and that is actually
a common theme in your dream

00:38:48.380 --> 00:38:50.560
and in Andrew's dream.

00:38:50.560 --> 00:38:53.320
And so let me ask
you, Andrew, in terms

00:38:53.320 --> 00:38:57.895
of this multi-party protocol
you have outlined for us,

00:38:57.895 --> 00:39:01.090
is it used today?

00:39:01.090 --> 00:39:05.277
In what feels like we
imagined using those ideas?

00:39:05.277 --> 00:39:07.360
Well, I mean, parts of it
are certainly used today

00:39:07.360 --> 00:39:10.420
because it's based upon ideas
that were pioneered by some

00:39:10.420 --> 00:39:13.510
of the early cryptographers,
folks at CSAIL,

00:39:13.510 --> 00:39:16.990
like Ron Rubesta, Shapi
Goblosser, Sylvia Tolley,

00:39:16.990 --> 00:39:19.570
these are giants in the
field, and they pioneered

00:39:19.570 --> 00:39:20.650
a lot of applications.

00:39:20.650 --> 00:39:22.870
When you use a credit
card transaction online,

00:39:22.870 --> 00:39:26.150
you're using part of these
kind of encryption methods.

00:39:26.150 --> 00:39:28.540
Secure multi-party
computation, though where

00:39:28.540 --> 00:39:31.690
you're allowing individuals
to encrypt their data

00:39:31.690 --> 00:39:33.130
and then share
the encrypted data

00:39:33.130 --> 00:39:35.800
to be able to compute with
it, that's really new.

00:39:35.800 --> 00:39:37.600
And part of the newness
comes from the fact

00:39:37.600 --> 00:39:39.640
that it's only been a
few years ago that we

00:39:39.640 --> 00:39:41.440
solved the problem
with so-called fully

00:39:41.440 --> 00:39:43.090
homomorphic encryption.

00:39:43.090 --> 00:39:44.620
That's a scary-sounding word.

00:39:44.620 --> 00:39:47.760
It simply means that you can
encrypt your private data,

00:39:47.760 --> 00:39:49.510
then give somebody
that encrypted data,

00:39:49.510 --> 00:39:52.840
and they can take that data
and compute certain statistics

00:39:52.840 --> 00:39:55.300
as if they had the unencrypted
data, but at no time

00:39:55.300 --> 00:39:57.520
can they access the
underlying data.

00:39:57.520 --> 00:39:59.620
And you can imagine the
applications are huge.

00:39:59.620 --> 00:40:01.800
For example, not just in
financial application,

00:40:01.800 --> 00:40:02.890
but health care.

00:40:02.890 --> 00:40:05.020
Imagine if we could share
all of our health care

00:40:05.020 --> 00:40:09.010
data to figure out what truly
causes cancer or diabetes,

00:40:09.010 --> 00:40:11.230
but without having
to disclose details

00:40:11.230 --> 00:40:13.603
about our own private
medical condition.

00:40:13.603 --> 00:40:15.020
That's an example
where I think it

00:40:15.020 --> 00:40:18.370
will transform our society if
we don't get the technology out

00:40:18.370 --> 00:40:20.240
there.

00:40:20.240 --> 00:40:22.810
So that's really
great, and it was

00:40:22.810 --> 00:40:26.890
very exciting to see
in your presentation

00:40:26.890 --> 00:40:31.900
the collaboration between
computer scientist and finance.

00:40:31.900 --> 00:40:35.200
And I just wonder if you can
elaborate just a little bit

00:40:35.200 --> 00:40:36.550
more on this.

00:40:36.550 --> 00:40:40.600
Over the past decade and even
before it into the next decade,

00:40:40.600 --> 00:40:45.700
how will computer
scientists and economists

00:40:45.700 --> 00:40:49.018
come together to solve even
more important problems?

00:40:49.018 --> 00:40:50.560
Well, you know more
and more, I think

00:40:50.560 --> 00:40:52.450
computer science
is actually getting

00:40:52.450 --> 00:40:55.870
involved in a number of
economic transactions.

