Getting away from that?

00:53:09.410 --> 00:53:11.750
The question is how do
you see that changing?

00:53:17.493 --> 00:53:20.070
I think it may change in
lots of different ways,

00:53:20.070 --> 00:53:21.980
and how it changes is up to us.

00:53:21.980 --> 00:53:24.390
It's not clear how
it's going to be.

00:53:24.390 --> 00:53:24.890
All right.

00:53:24.890 --> 00:53:25.515
Thank you, Tim.

00:53:25.515 --> 00:53:27.150
And next question.

00:53:27.150 --> 00:53:28.750
Thank you so much.

00:53:28.750 --> 00:53:30.430
Can you describe a
bit about the future

00:53:30.430 --> 00:53:32.752
as far as the type
and nature of jobs

00:53:32.752 --> 00:53:34.460
that will exist when
the future of robots

00:53:34.460 --> 00:53:36.620
become well and
truly ubiquitous?

00:53:36.620 --> 00:53:38.540
Could you speak on that a bit?

00:53:38.540 --> 00:53:40.260
So I had to--

00:53:40.260 --> 00:53:42.020
maybe Daniela is
also robotics, so

00:53:42.020 --> 00:53:44.240
let me let her kill it first.

00:53:44.240 --> 00:53:48.380
So we're very
excited about working

00:53:48.380 --> 00:53:51.160
on the next generation
of robots that will

00:53:51.160 --> 00:53:54.170
that will help people
in manufacturing,

00:53:54.170 --> 00:53:57.950
and that will bring
manufacturing back to the US.

00:53:57.950 --> 00:54:01.760
And we imagine a
world where workers

00:54:01.760 --> 00:54:07.550
will be able to synthesize
the robots for any task using

00:54:07.550 --> 00:54:11.967
computational tools and easy
interfaces, a kind of a robot

00:54:11.967 --> 00:54:13.550
keep costs, if you
will, where instead

00:54:13.550 --> 00:54:16.710
of printing your poster, you
will be printing your robot

00:54:16.710 --> 00:54:19.040
to solve that particular
task on demand

00:54:19.040 --> 00:54:21.930
at the time when
you need a task.

00:54:21.930 --> 00:54:26.270
But the robot cannot be fully
in control of a factory.

00:54:26.270 --> 00:54:28.580
People will still be in charge.

00:54:28.580 --> 00:54:31.790
The robots will be
available to help people.

00:54:31.790 --> 00:54:39.290
And in my dream, I would like
to see manufacturing environment

00:54:39.290 --> 00:54:42.330
where we change from
design in the US

00:54:42.330 --> 00:54:46.200
and fabricated in China
to design in the US,

00:54:46.200 --> 00:54:49.280
customize at home in your
living room by yourself

00:54:49.280 --> 00:54:51.650
and fabricate it locally.

00:54:51.650 --> 00:54:52.650
I totally agree.

00:54:52.650 --> 00:54:54.590
So in the first
robot revolution,

00:54:54.590 --> 00:54:56.510
happened right around
the 1980s where

00:54:56.510 --> 00:54:59.630
the PUMA robots came
out and revolutionized

00:54:59.630 --> 00:55:01.090
factory robotics.

00:55:01.090 --> 00:55:02.840
And if you look at the
automotive industry

00:55:02.840 --> 00:55:06.470
just before that happened
and just after it happened,

00:55:06.470 --> 00:55:09.050
we were producing basically
after 5 or 10 years,

00:55:09.050 --> 00:55:11.360
we were producing
twice as many cars,

00:55:11.360 --> 00:55:14.540
but we were also
employing 15% more people

00:55:14.540 --> 00:55:15.830
to make those cars work.

00:55:15.830 --> 00:55:19.160
So every time we've added
a huge technology leap,

00:55:19.160 --> 00:55:20.510
it's been good for people.

00:55:20.510 --> 00:55:21.950
It's been good for jobs.

00:55:21.950 --> 00:55:24.980
The challenge is going to be
educating people so that they

00:55:24.980 --> 00:55:26.360
can work with the robots.

00:55:26.360 --> 00:55:29.540
And the next level of
technology leap, we

00:55:29.540 --> 00:55:31.450
have to work right into
the education system.

00:55:31.450 --> 00:55:34.130
We have to teach people computer
science and robotics when

00:55:34.130 --> 00:55:35.040
they're young.

00:55:35.040 --> 00:55:37.820
And the Dean Kamen,
the FIRST robotics team

00:55:37.820 --> 00:55:40.850
is here at South by
Southwest, and I just

00:55:40.850 --> 00:55:42.920
think this is so
important for the future

00:55:42.920 --> 00:55:46.040
to make this transition happen.

00:55:46.040 --> 00:55:47.850
Thank you for that question.

00:55:47.850 --> 00:55:50.140
Next question.

00:55:50.140 --> 00:55:51.010
Hi.

00:55:51.010 --> 00:55:52.260
Good morning.

00:55:52.260 --> 00:55:53.990
I have a follow-up
question on that,

00:55:53.990 --> 00:55:55.960
and then a question
on internet policy.

00:55:55.960 --> 00:56:00.790
So my question on the
previous question was,

00:56:00.790 --> 00:56:04.630
do you see that as we start
introducing technology

00:56:04.630 --> 00:56:05.760
like driverless cars--

00:56:05.760 --> 00:56:06.442
Hold the mic.

00:56:06.442 --> 00:56:07.900
As we start to
introduce technology

00:56:07.900 --> 00:56:10.330
like driverless cars,
you can very much

00:56:10.330 --> 00:56:13.630
see where the taxi industry
might become quickly obsolete

00:56:13.630 --> 00:56:15.310
once that happens.

00:56:15.310 --> 00:56:19.510
How do you see markets
where manual labor

00:56:19.510 --> 00:56:22.990
or entry-level jobs will
be affected by robotics

00:56:22.990 --> 00:56:25.925
and how that might change?

00:56:25.925 --> 00:56:26.800
I think you're right.

00:56:26.800 --> 00:56:27.740
I think that's a tough question.

00:56:27.740 --> 00:56:29.890
I think people's jobs
will ultimately change.

00:56:29.890 --> 00:56:33.520
But I think in the short
term, it's important for us

00:56:33.520 --> 00:56:37.180
to embrace the
technology, make policies

00:56:37.180 --> 00:56:40.810
so that people can explore this
technology, and educate people

00:56:40.810 --> 00:56:42.200
so they can make the transition.

00:56:42.200 --> 00:56:47.500
But I think some jobs
will inevitably change.

00:56:47.500 --> 00:56:50.930
I think self-driving cars
are going to take time.

00:56:50.930 --> 00:56:54.330
They're not as close as you
might feel watching YouTube.

00:56:54.330 --> 00:56:57.970
But I hope that I'll be
reading a book next time when

00:56:57.970 --> 00:57:00.662
I'm in a car in 20 years
instead of being stressed out

00:57:00.662 --> 00:57:01.370
behind the wheel.

00:57:01.370 --> 00:57:04.960
Can I just jump
in to re emphasize

00:57:04.960 --> 00:57:10.830
that the jobs will not change
with the flip of a button.

00:57:10.830 --> 00:57:12.450
They don't change instantly.

00:57:12.450 --> 00:57:16.020
It will take some time,
and so the question

00:57:16.020 --> 00:57:20.010
is, how do we retrain to go
along with this transition.

00:57:20.010 --> 00:57:21.750
And hopefully, during
this transition,

00:57:21.750 --> 00:57:25.740
the jobs will become more
exciting and more interesting

00:57:25.740 --> 00:57:27.320
for all of us.

00:57:27.320 --> 00:57:31.130
Just like what you experience
with the agriculture revolution

00:57:31.130 --> 00:57:33.710
and with industrial revolution.

00:57:33.710 --> 00:57:35.260
Excellent.

00:57:35.260 --> 00:57:38.070
All of that, I think
would be worthwhile

00:57:38.070 --> 00:57:41.820
thinking as a community, how
can we institute training

00:57:41.820 --> 00:57:46.760
for people who are just entering
the job market on how they can

00:57:46.760 --> 00:57:49.580
move on from a market that
perhaps is being replaced

00:57:49.580 --> 00:57:53.240
by newer technology and help
them in their transition.

00:57:53.240 --> 00:57:55.850
So that's a good question,
and here's the dream.

00:57:55.850 --> 00:57:58.317
What if every high school
kid in this country

00:57:58.317 --> 00:57:59.900
learned how to program
in high school.

00:57:59.900 --> 00:58:01.060
Wouldn't that be cool?

00:58:01.060 --> 00:58:01.560
Yes.

00:58:01.560 --> 00:58:02.930
I'm a big proponent of that.

00:58:02.930 --> 00:58:07.340
It's just as important as
reading and arithmetic.

00:58:07.340 --> 00:58:10.220
Not just because we
need more programmers,

00:58:10.220 --> 00:58:13.870
but because we need the people
who are lawyers and politicians

00:58:13.870 --> 00:58:16.698
to know how to program so that
they can understand what's

00:58:16.698 --> 00:58:17.740
possible with technology.

00:58:17.740 --> 00:58:18.480
Exactly.

00:58:18.480 --> 00:58:21.200
So in the 21st
century, I believe

00:58:21.200 --> 00:58:24.570
that programming is as
important as reading, writing,

00:58:24.570 --> 00:58:27.790
and calculating, doing math.

00:58:27.790 --> 00:58:29.310
Yes.

00:58:29.310 --> 00:58:30.778
The other question was--

00:58:30.778 --> 00:58:32.320
We have some Trojans
in the audience.

00:58:32.320 --> 00:58:35.120
It's very important
for lawyers as well,

00:58:35.120 --> 00:58:38.395
but to have a foundation of
technology and as a manager

00:58:38.395 --> 00:58:40.520
as well, I think it's really
important for managers

00:58:40.520 --> 00:58:43.710
also to understand technology
and working with technology.

00:58:43.710 --> 00:58:44.880
You should keep it.

00:58:44.880 --> 00:58:45.530
All right.

00:58:45.530 --> 00:58:47.820
So my follow-up question was--

00:58:47.820 --> 00:58:50.090
Why don't you join
the back of the queue.

00:58:50.090 --> 00:58:52.750
We have time.

00:58:52.750 --> 00:58:54.260
We'll get to you again.

00:58:54.260 --> 00:58:54.830
Hi.

00:58:54.830 --> 00:58:56.720
Thank you very much
for the [INAUDIBLE]..

00:58:56.720 --> 00:58:59.900
So to Professor Lo touched
on the subject a little bit

00:58:59.900 --> 00:59:03.290
with the financial
data, but my question

00:59:03.290 --> 00:59:05.420
is, do you think we
can actually make

00:59:05.420 --> 00:59:08.870
a balance between censorship
and privacy and sometimes could

00:59:08.870 --> 00:59:12.900
they conflict, and
who should do this?

00:59:12.900 --> 00:59:20.308
Who should be the agency or
media to make this balance?

00:59:20.308 --> 00:59:22.100
So I think that's a
very important question

00:59:22.100 --> 00:59:25.130
that you raised, and I was
actually going to bring it up

00:59:25.130 --> 00:59:28.430
when Tim was talking a
bit about the data that is

00:59:28.430 --> 00:59:30.740
on the internet all about him.

00:59:30.740 --> 00:59:32.390
I think that there's
a lot of benefits

00:59:32.390 --> 00:59:34.520
to having all of
your data out there,

00:59:34.520 --> 00:59:38.430
but your question really
is about potential costs.

00:59:38.430 --> 00:59:40.370
And let me give you one
example, and then I'll

00:59:40.370 --> 00:59:43.280
tell you what I think
you can do about it.

00:59:43.280 --> 00:59:45.440
If I were an
insurance company, I

00:59:45.440 --> 00:59:47.570
would love to get
that data that Tim

00:59:47.570 --> 00:59:50.150
has on his app with
his accelerometer.

00:59:50.150 --> 00:59:52.730
Because I'd like to
know how fast he drives.

00:59:52.730 --> 00:59:54.740
I'd like to know where he goes.

00:59:54.740 --> 00:59:56.990
I'd like to know what
his health condition is

00:59:56.990 --> 00:59:59.330
because once I find out
all that information,

00:59:59.330 --> 01:00:02.090
I may decide to
refuse to insure him.

01:00:02.090 --> 01:00:03.490
Because he's not a good risk.

01:00:03.490 --> 01:00:04.500
No way, buddy.

01:00:07.470 --> 01:00:09.210
And this is the problem.

01:00:09.210 --> 01:00:11.570
It's that when your
data is out there,

01:00:11.570 --> 01:00:14.300
it can be used in ways
that are unanticipated.

01:00:14.300 --> 01:00:16.910
And this is why I think Tim's
point about new legislation

01:00:16.910 --> 01:00:17.900
is so critical.

01:00:17.900 --> 01:00:20.750
We don't have legislation
to protect our privacy

01:00:20.750 --> 01:00:22.970
and to prevent
the misuse of data

01:00:22.970 --> 01:00:25.340
because, frankly, when the
founders of our country

01:00:25.340 --> 01:00:28.010
were framing the
Constitution, they

01:00:28.010 --> 01:00:31.590
didn't really understand the
notion of data and privacy.

01:00:31.590 --> 01:00:34.880
So I think that with better
legislation-- and legislation

01:00:34.880 --> 01:00:36.650
is going to have to
come from the people.

01:00:36.650 --> 01:00:38.942
You're going to have to write
your congressman and say,

01:00:38.942 --> 01:00:40.100
this is an important issue.

01:00:40.100 --> 01:00:42.855
We want you to be at the
front of it and not in back.

01:00:42.855 --> 01:00:45.230
I think that that's going to
be a critical part of what's

01:00:45.230 --> 01:00:47.790
going to happen over
the next 5 to 10 years.

01:00:47.790 --> 01:00:50.240
So you think someone
should be able to sensor

01:00:50.240 --> 01:00:54.930
data and software because it
violates someone's privacy?

01:00:54.930 --> 01:00:58.730
I think that censorship is
very different from preserving

01:00:58.730 --> 01:01:01.980
privacy, and I think this has
to do with individual rights.

01:01:01.980 --> 01:01:04.430
In other words,
you have the right

01:01:04.430 --> 01:01:07.460
not to share your data
with me, but nobody

01:01:07.460 --> 01:01:10.190
should have the right to
limit our free speech.

01:01:10.190 --> 01:01:11.700
Those are two very
different things.

01:01:11.700 --> 01:01:13.640
And I think that right
now the law does not

01:01:13.640 --> 01:01:16.820
reflect that distinction, and we
need to have better legislation

01:01:16.820 --> 01:01:18.095
to deal with those issues.

01:01:18.095 --> 01:01:18.680
Thank you.

01:01:18.680 --> 01:01:20.090
So thank you for that question.

01:01:20.090 --> 01:01:22.580
We're officially
out of time, so let

01:01:22.580 --> 01:01:25.310
me suggest that those of you
who haven't had their questions

01:01:25.310 --> 01:01:29.000
answered, join us at the podium,
and before that, let's thank

01:01:29.000 --> 01:01:30.450
the speakers one more time.

01:01:33.150 --> 01:01:36.150
And also thank you for
those who were online.

01:01:36.150 --> 01:01:39.500
[INAUDIBLE]

